**Addressing Blank Pages**

I'm currently focused on the blank pages issue when printing certificates. My initial thought is browser-related; perhaps margins or overflowing elements are the culprits, inadvertently creating blank pages. I'm leaning towards inspecting CSS and print settings to pinpoint the cause and determine how to eliminate these unwanted blank pages in the printing process.


**Refining CSS Adjustments**

I'm now diving back into the CSS, specifically within `index.html`. My focus is on ensuring margins are absolutely zeroed out for printing. I'm going to implement `@page { size: landscape; margin: 0 !important; }` and `body { margin: 0 !important; padding: 0 !important; }` in `@media print`. I also want to make sure I'm correctly setting `height: 100vh` and using `break-after: page` for the certificate pages. Finally, I'll pay close attention to the last page and how breaks are handled. I need to make sure I avoid unwanted page breaks at the end, so I'll check `.certificate-print-content > .certificate-page:last-child`.


**Optimizing Print Layout**

I'm now revising my `index.html` file, specifically the `@media print` block. I've updated the `@page` directive to enforce zero margins and set the size to landscape. For `.certificate-page`, I'm setting `height` to `100vh` and adding `width: 100vw; overflow: hidden; page-break-after: always; break-after: page; margin: 0; padding: 0;` to ensure each certificate fits a single page without unintended overflow. I'm focusing on ensuring content within these pages doesn't have large internal margins, which could still create overflow, so that `100vh` with zero margins results in a perfect fit.


**Finalizing Print Configuration**

I'm now implementing the print-specific CSS rules in detail. I'm focusing on ensuring zero margins on the `body` and the `@page` directive. I've also set the width and height of the `body` to 100%. I'm ensuring `page-break-after: always` and `break-after: page` are correctly applied to `certificate-page` for proper page breaks. Finally, I've added a specific rule to auto-break the last page.


**Confirming HTML Implementation**

I'm now integrating the print-specific CSS into the `index.html` file, referencing the user's focus on eliminating blank pages. I've noted the user's re-emphasis on the issue, which validates the need for a thorough solution. I'm focusing on zeroing out margins in the `@page` directive, which will be the central fix. I'll ensure the CSS includes the landscape orientation and proper page breaks, based on the previous responses. I'm also confirming the landscape layout is correct based on the UI.


**Eliminating Overflow Issues**

My focus is now on confirming that the `@page` margin fix solves the overflow. I've pinpointed the culprit: the previous 0.5cm margin in the `@page` combined with `height: 100vh` on the `.certificate-page`. This caused content to overflow to a blank page. Setting `@page { margin: 0 !important; }` and `.certificate-page { overflow: hidden; }` should resolve this. I'll also ensure the signature swap from the original design is still implemented and the design looks perfect.


**Implementing Signature Updates**

I'm now integrating the signature swap. In the JSX, I'm adjusting the positions, ensuring "مدير فريق مكافحة العدوى" appears on the right side and "مدير عام المستشفى" on the left as the Hospital Manager, with the Infection Control Manager now first. I'm also planning to address the excessive spacing in the content body by reducing the `flex-grow` value to improve the layout and ensure a compact presentation during printing. I'm focusing on those adjustments in the `index.html` file.


**Verifying Text Direction**

I'm now confirming the text direction alignment within the signature elements. My understanding is that, since `dir="rtl"`, the current positions of the Infection Control Manager and Hospital Manager are correct; the first element is on the right, and second on the left. I'm double-checking the visual output to confirm that the names and titles are correctly positioned, as expected, within the RTL layout. The goal is to ensure the UI design is accurate and aligns with the expected document flow.


**Validating RTL Alignment**

I'm now focusing on validating the right-to-left text flow and confirming signature placement within the current code structure. Since "مدير فريق مكافحة العدوى" (Infection Control Manager) appears first in the HTML, and the `dir="rtl"` attribute is in use, I'm verifying that the Infection Control Manager is indeed positioned on the right side of the certificate and the Hospital Manager is on the left. I'll thoroughly inspect the visual output.


**Confirming Single-Page Layout**

I'm now focusing on the core requirement: ensuring each trainee's certificate prints on a separate page. I will concentrate on the `<certificate-page>` elements in the HTML and confirm that the page breaks are applied correctly, specifically using `page-break-after: always` and `break-after: page`. I'll also double-check that the margins are correctly set to zero, to avoid unexpected page overflows.


**Resolving Blank Page Glitches**

I'm revisiting the blank page problem, but now I need to check the certificate design closely. I'm checking page breaks and margins. I'm making sure that signature adjustments, made in a previous commit, didn't introduce any layout problems related to the new signature positions. I'm ensuring everything flows onto a single page without unwanted blank pages.


**Verifying Signature Order**

I'm now checking the JSX code and confirming the order of the signature blocks, given the `dir="rtl"` attribute. Based on the HTML, "مدير فريق مكافحة العدوى" (Infection Control Manager) comes first. Since the `dir="rtl"` attribute is set, I'll confirm that the Infection Control Manager's signature is positioned on the right side of the certificate and the Hospital Manager's signature on the left. The `index.html` file appears to have the order implemented.


**Refining Page Break Logic**

I'm now implementing the print CSS fixes I've identified as critical to fixing the blank page problem. I'm focusing on zeroing out the margins in `@page` and the `body` element. I'm also confirming `height: 100vh`, `width: 100%`, and `overflow: hidden` are in place for the `.certificate-page` elements. To ensure a single certificate per page, I'm setting `break-after: page` and `page-break-after: always`.


**Prioritizing Content Presentation**

I'm now considering the best way to keep the footer at the bottom of the page, even if the content is short, while still ensuring no overflow occurs. The current file sets `justify-content: flex-start` on `.certificate-page`, which, combined with the short content, results in the footer appearing high on the page. I'm focusing on strictly resolving print issues before fine-tuning the layout and ensuring the certificate prints within a single page.


**Implementing Print Adjustments**

I'm now integrating all of the print-specific CSS rules into the `index.html` file, including those related to page size, margins, and layout. I'm focusing specifically on zeroing out the margins to prevent blank pages. I'm confirming that the body width and height are set to 100% to ensure that the content fits the entire print page. I am updating my existing print style section.

```css
            {
                margin: 0 !important;
                padding: 0 !important;
                width: 100% !important;
                height: 100% !important;
                overflow: hidden !important; /* Ensure no overflow */
            }

            .certificate-print-content {
                width: 100%;
                height: 100%;
            }

            .certificate-page {
                width: 100vw;
                height: 100vh;
                margin: 0 !important;
                padding: 0 !important;
                overflow: hidden !important; /* Prevent content overflow */
                page-break-after: always;
                break-after: page; /* Ensure a page break after each certificate */
            }

            /* Optional: Force break after last certificate if needed */
            .certificate-print-content > .certificate-page:last-child {
                break-after: always;
            }
        }
```


**Enhancing Print Visibility**

I'm now incorporating print-specific display rules. The goal is to ensure only the certificates print and to hide any unnecessary elements using `body.print-mode-certificates .normal-print-content { display: none !important; }` and `body.print-mode-certificates .certificate-print-content { display: block !important; }`. I'm using `display: block !important;` for elements and `display: table-row !important;` for table rows within the print view, for print visibility and adjustment of the layout, along with setting up print-only visibility.


**Refining Print Display**

I'm now focusing on controlling the visibility of elements during printing. I'm leveraging CSS rules to ensure only certificates are visible when printing, hiding other content using `display: none !important;` for the unnecessary elements. I'm confirming that the `certificate-page` has the proper display value of block. I've also focused on visibility adjustments to the tables in order to make sure the tables are visible when printing.



**Adjusting Print Header**

I'm now refining the print display by incorporating print-specific CSS rules. My aim is to set `page-break-after` and `break-after` to `auto !important` for the certificate pages, with a focus on customizing the certificate header for print. I'm eliminating the bottom border and padding, hiding the logo, and making sure the title has a large font size. I'm also ensuring the text is right-aligned for an optimal print output.

I am also making the certificate body more compact by setting font size and line height.


**Optimizing Print Layout Details**

I am now focusing on the print layout, specifically to address the list formatting, ensuring the content is optimally presented. I'm focusing on the `.policy-list` elements to enhance readability and ensure the content fits within a single page, and I have found the `flex-grow: 1;` is causing excessive spacing. I'm focusing on the `column-count`, `column-gap`, and list element formatting. Finally, I'm adjusting the footer's spacing to keep it consistent.

